# Golden set for the CURATOR bake-off (packages/obscura-web/src/bench-curate.mjs), distinct from
# queries.jsonl (which scores candidate RANKING, not curation — see bench-research.mjs's own header
# for why the two need separate scorers: this one needs a live Ollama, so it cannot be a
# deterministic CI gate the way the ranking bench is).
#
# Format: {query, url, label: "relevant"|"irrelevant", note?}. `url` keys a frozen page-text
# fixture in curation-fixtures/<hash8>.txt (hash8 from url-identity.mjs, the same convention
# RESEARCH/ notes use) captured by bench-curate-capture.mjs.
#
# Every entry here is real and lived through this session's own live pipeline runs — none invented:
#
# - The three `relevant` SQLite pages and the `hard-negative` MDN pages are the EXACT case a live
#   run of this pipeline hit: querying "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking" with the default
#   curator (phi4-mini) scored 6 MDN WritableStreamDefaultWriter pages `relevance:9` (the maximum)
#   — almost certainly fooled by literal "writer"/"lock" overlap with a completely unrelated
#   JavaScript Streams API. That live failure is why this bench exists at all.
# - `easy-negative` is a sanity floor: a curator that scores THIS as relevant is broken outright,
#   not just fooled by keyword overlap — any candidate model failing it is disqualified regardless
#   of its score on the harder cases.
# - The second query is the `vocab-mismatch` case from queries.jsonl's own philosophy: same page,
#   colloquial Spanish wording sharing almost no vocabulary with it — a curator that only pattern
#   -matches surface words should do WORSE here than on the first query's technical phrasing.

{"query": "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking", "url": "https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html", "label": "relevant", "note": "the primary source on WAL mode itself"}
{"query": "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking", "url": "https://www.sqlite.org/lockingv3.html", "label": "relevant", "note": "SQLite's own locking model doc"}
{"query": "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking", "url": "https://jellyfin.org/posts/SQLite-locking/", "label": "relevant", "note": "a real-world writeup of hitting this exact issue"}
{"query": "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking", "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WritableStreamDefaultWriter", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "hard-negative: the confirmed live false-positive (scored relevance:9 by phi4-mini)"}
{"query": "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking", "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WritableStreamDefaultWriter/write", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "hard-negative: same MDN family, same confirmed live false-positive"}
{"query": "SQLite WAL concurrent writer locking", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banana", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "easy-negative sanity floor: failing this means broken, not just fooled"}

{"query": "se me traba sqlite cuando dos procesos escriben a la vez", "url": "https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html", "label": "relevant", "note": "vocab-mismatch: same answer page, colloquial Spanish query sharing almost no vocabulary with it"}
{"query": "se me traba sqlite cuando dos procesos escriben a la vez", "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WritableStreamDefaultWriter", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "same hard-negative, under the vocab-mismatch query"}

# ── Acronym-collision hard negatives (live, RESEARCH/winoptengine-v19-candidates, 2026-07-27) ──
# A deep-research run about message-signalled interrupts emitted queries carrying the bare acronyms
# "MSI" and "MSIX". Both are correct terms; both are also something else entirely (MSI the hardware
# brand, MSIX the Windows packaging format). SearXNG returned real, well-ranked pages for the OTHER
# meaning and the curator scored several of them 8-9 — not unreasonably, since relative to a query
# that literally says "MSI" they are not obviously wrong.
#
# These are recorded as curator cases so the bake-off can see them, but the honest expectation is
# that the CURATOR cannot win them all: with an ambiguous query the judge has no evidence of intent.
# The real fix is upstream, in EXPAND_SYSTEM_PROMPT's disambiguation rule. Keep them here anyway —
# they are the regression that proves the upstream fix is still working.
{"query": "msix vs pci interrupts gpu nvme performance bottleneck evidence", "url": "https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/decreto-lei/del2848.htm", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "hard-negative: Brazilian penal code, scored relevance:9 live. Acronym collision, not a judgment failure"}
{"query": "cpu core parking disable impact dpc latency vs boot time metric", "url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unidad_central_de_procesamiento", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "hard-negative: Wikipedia 'what is a CPU', scored relevance:9 live. Shares 'cpu' and nothing else"}
{"query": "cpu core parking disable impact dpc latency vs boot time metric", "url": "https://www.cpuid.com/softwares/cpu-z.html", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "hard-negative: CPU-Z version history, scored relevance:9 live. A product page about CPUs, not about parking"}
{"query": "windows ssd write cache controller settings registry", "url": "https://www.osforensics.com/whats-new.html", "label": "irrelevant", "note": "hard-negative: OSForensics changelog, scored relevance:9 live with the reason 'Directly addresses querying Windows SSD write cache controller settings registry' — a reason that is simply false"}
